<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Partition of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Third Partition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was the final division of the remaining territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1795. It followed the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadeusz-kosciuszko">Tadeusz Kościuszko</a>'s insurrection of 1794. Accounts of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth treat it as the end of the state, which ceased to exist as an independent polity until Polish independence was restored in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland.jpg" alt="Third Partition of Poland" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Halibutt; derivative work by Sneecs · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partitions_of_Poland.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition</a> of 1772, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a> tried to strengthen the weakened Commonwealth by reforms that reached their climax in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>, which established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with three branches of government and abolished many privileges of the nobility. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, angered by what it saw as dangerous Jacobin-style reforms, invaded in 1792.</p><p>Abandoned by its Prussian allies and betrayed by Polish nobles who wanted their privileges back, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was forced to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> in 1793, which gave Dobrzyn, Kujavia and much of Greater Poland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and the eastern provinces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. On 24 March 1794 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadeusz-kosciuszko">Tadeusz Kościuszko</a> took command of the Polish armed forces and declared a national uprising. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> and Frederick William II responded, and the uprising was crushed by November 1794. According to legend, when Kościuszko fell from his horse at the Battle of Maciejowice shortly before his capture, he said Finis Poloniae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Austrian, Prussian and Russian representatives met on 24 October 1795 to dissolve the Commonwealth, and the three powers signed a treaty dividing the territory on 26 January 1797. 2 million people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podlachia">Podlachia</a>, the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, with 1 million people. 2 million people. Unlike in the earlier partitions, no Polish representative took part in the treaty.</p><p>King Stanisław was forced to abdicate and retire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>, where he died in 1798. The powers also agreed never to include the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> in their titles, so that the name 'shall remain suppressed'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The partition ended the existence of independent Polish or Lithuanian states for 123 years. Many politicians, intellectuals and revolutionaries were forced to leave, in what was later called the Great Emigration, and many served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the Polish Legions. Polish poets and artists made the desire for national freedom a mark of the Polish Romanticist movement.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> created the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1807, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 replaced it with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, whose autonomy was largely ended after an insurrection in 1831. In 1867 Russia made it an official part of the empire. Full independence returned only at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and on 29 August 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> of the Russian republic denounced the partitions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Partition of Poland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It led directly to the <b>Third Partition</b> of 1795, which erased the Commonwealth from the map of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…wrote Poland Is Not Yet Lost for the Polish Legions in the French army, two years after the <b>third partition</b> had erased the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth from the map; its idea was that a nation could exist without a state as long as its people lived and fought for…</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>His speech is considered the start of the Kościuszko Uprising, whose defeat led to the <b>Third Partition</b> of 1795.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-24">24 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>With the <b>Third Partition of Poland</b> in 1795, Pilten was annexed by Russia together with Courland and absorbed into the Governorate of Courland.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-pilten">District of Pilten</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Third Partition of Poland</b> in 1795, the Courland nobility submitted to Catherine the Great, and the duchy was annexed by Russia, becoming the Governorate of Courland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Third Partition</b> in 1795 it passed to the Kingdom of Prussia, and later to the Duchy of Warsaw and Congress Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-siewierz">Duchy of Siewierz</a></li></ul></section>
